WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:08.040
Sugar made in a leaf ends up in a root below it and a shoot tip above it.

2
00:00:09.020 --> 00:00:12.060
What moves it both ways, with no heart and nothing pulling?

3
00:00:13.233 --> 00:00:15.973
Water in the xylem is pulled toward dry air.

4
00:00:17.353 --> 00:00:20.033
Sugar rides in the phloem, in living sieve tubes.

5
00:00:20.893 --> 00:00:23.473
And nothing evaporates at a root to pull it.

6
00:00:24.417 --> 00:00:28.737
In 1930, Ernst Münch pictured two bulbs joined by a tube.

7
00:00:30.017 --> 00:00:34.097
Each is wrapped in a membrane that lets water through but holds sugar back.

8
00:00:35.417 --> 00:00:36.417
Both sit in water.

9
00:00:37.450 --> 00:00:39.970
Put strong sugar solution in one bulb.

10
00:00:41.210 --> 00:00:45.790
The sugar lowers its water potential, so water rushes in by osmosis.

11
00:00:47.130 --> 00:00:48.530
The pressure inside rises.

12
00:00:49.683 --> 00:00:53.843
That pressure pushes the solution along the tube toward the other bulb.

13
00:00:55.043 --> 00:00:57.963
The sap flows in bulk, from higher pressure to lower.

14
00:00:59.117 --> 00:01:02.957
In the plant, a leaf making sugar is the first bulb, the source.

15
00:01:04.257 --> 00:01:08.497
A root, a fruit or a shoot tip using sugar is the second bulb, a sink.

16
00:01:09.567 --> 00:01:13.707
At a sink, sugar is removed, water leaves, and the pressure falls.

17
00:01:15.067 --> 00:01:18.007
The xylem alongside supplies the water at the source.

18
00:01:19.150 --> 00:01:22.410
Sap flows from source to sink, wherever the sink sits.

19
00:01:23.350 --> 00:01:26.770
So it can run down to the roots or up to a growing shoot tip.

20
00:01:27.783 --> 00:01:29.843
For decades there was an objection.

21
00:01:31.403 --> 00:01:36.203
Sieve tubes are narrow, and blocked at intervals by perforated end walls, the sieve

22
00:01:36.203 --> 00:01:41.563
plates. Pushing sap through metres of them seemed to need impossible pressures.

23
00:01:42.733 --> 00:01:48.593
In 2016, Michael Knoblauch, Michele Holbrook and colleagues tested this in the

24
00:01:48.593 --> 00:01:54.173
morning glory. They measured the pressure inside working sieve tubes with tiny

25
00:01:54.173 --> 00:01:55.173
probes.

26
00:01:55.717 --> 00:02:00.057
Then they made the sugar's path longer, by removing leaves from long vines.

27
00:02:01.557 --> 00:02:04.417
Now the sugar had to travel much farther to reach the roots.

28
00:02:05.517 --> 00:02:08.717
With the same narrow tubes, the pressure needed would soar.

29
00:02:10.057 --> 00:02:14.157
If you make the path much longer, what must change for the sap still to arrive?

30
00:02:20.300 --> 00:02:21.860
Two things changed together.

31
00:02:23.140 --> 00:02:25.520
First, source pressure rose with path length.

32
00:02:26.680 --> 00:02:29.800
It passed 2.2 MPa in the longest vine.

33
00:02:30.933 --> 00:02:36.293
Second, the tubes conducted five to six times better, mainly because the sieve-plate

34
00:02:36.293 --> 00:02:42.293
pores were wider. With both changes, the estimated reach of pressure flow matched

35
00:02:42.293 --> 00:02:43.293
the length of the vine.

36
00:02:44.367 --> 00:02:46.867
That is one species, measured with care.

37
00:02:48.187 --> 00:02:51.887
The authors read it as strong support for pressure flow in this plant.

38
00:02:53.000 --> 00:02:56.620
Where sugar goes in, water follows and the pressure rises.

39
00:02:58.220 --> 00:03:00.480
Sap is pushed to wherever sugar is taken out.
